# ============================================================================
#  The native projects are GENERATED, and deliberately not committed.
# ============================================================================
#
#  `npx expo prebuild` writes android/ (and ios/) from app.json and the Expo
#  config plugins. Keeping them out of git is what preserves the reason we
#  chose Expo in the first place: app.json stays the single source of truth
#  for the app name, icon, permissions, scheme and SDK versions, and one
#  command regenerates both platforms from it.
#
#  Commit them the day we need a native change Expo cannot express — a custom
#  Gradle dependency, a hand-edited manifest, a native module of our own. That
#  is a real decision with a real cost (from then on, every Expo config change
#  is ours to apply by hand, on two platforms), and it should be made
#  deliberately rather than by accidentally running `git add .` after a build.
#
#  To rebuild the native project from scratch at any time:
#      cd apps/mobile
#      npx expo prebuild --clean
#
#  8 Sept 2026 — Amit's call: Android Studio for building and debugging, Expo
#  still managing the app itself.

android/
ios/

# Build and tooling output
.expo/
.gradle/
local.properties
*.keystore
!debug.keystore
*.jks

# Signing material must never reach the repository. A release keystore is not
# recoverable: lose it and the app can never be updated under the same
# identity again, and anyone who has it can publish as us. It belongs in a
# password manager and in EAS, nowhere else.
keystore.properties

# Firebase config. Not a secret - it ships inside the app - but it is
# per-environment, and a committed one is how a debug build ends up reporting
# into production. Supply it per build.
google-services.json
